hi there steve here uh if you like my  videos please subscribe i want to talk  about patterns how important they are  to  being able to speak comfortably easily  naturally in a in a foreign language  it's the key you have to master these  patterns and i find that you master them  best through lots of exposure lots of  repetition and paying attention and not  asking too many questions  and the reason i come to this is because  i have a video series with hana  for  koreans who are learning english and  i'll leave a link here in the  description box  and hannah asked why in english do we  say go home rather than go to home  or at home rather than at the home  and she had found an explanation that  said that in go home home becomes an  adverb whereas otherwise it's a noun to  me that's an explanation after the fact  what probably happened is that that  became the common way of saying things  and that's like so much in language  it's not a matter of you know because  the danger with grammatical explanations  is that you create the mind in the mind  of the learner that there's some great  logical mathematical logic behind  the rules of grammar there isn't the  rules of grammar just describe what  happens and so very often for example in  english where a double negative is a  no-no you can't say i don't know nothing  and they all you know a double negative  equals a positive and so they introduce  some sense of mathematics here whereas  in many languages double negatives are  exactly what is used in the slavic  languages for example  uh  in fact i often forget to use the double  negative  and then i figure that's good enough and  but i actually have to answer  you have to double negative and there's  no logic that's just how things are done  and in the slavic languages  oh and by the way i'm learning greek  right now but  the month of june is going to be my  slavic month i'm gonna be in bratislava  for the polyglot conference  and then i will be in ukraine for about  three weeks and all but in between i  have to go to vienna on business so then  i'm gonna concentrate on my slavic  languages which are still jumbled around  in my brain  also by the way for our miniseries we  need more  volunteers if possible to translate and  record in polish czech ukrainian  romanian hungarian in other words the  languages of that area that surround  bratislava but getting back to patterns  so  we develop these habits when we're with  one language through a lot of listening  and reading and then if we go to a  related language where the pattern is  different it's sometimes difficult i  found it difficult after doing czech and  ukrainian  polish then i go back to russian  and russian uses the verb to be is a lot  less than these other languages  there are there are many people  this book not this is a book  and  in czech and polish uh  for the verb to be is  they have a plural there are you know  they are they those people are  but in in russian they often leave the  is out  or in uh  ukrainian they only have the singular  they don't have the plural so as you're  in now sort of a slavic  pattern  mood and then you come across these  differences from one language to another  it sometimes throws you off  and this can happen in other languages  like in  in portuguese where they have this  personal infinitive  personal portuguese that reminds me we  would like some more volunteers for  european portuguese to record in  european portuguese we have lots for  brazilian portuguese if you're watching  and you're a speaker of  european portuguese please let me know  at any rate in portuguese they have this  personal infinitive which kind of  replaces the subjunctive to some extent  and that doesn't exist in spanish but  what i'm finding now very interesting in  greek  is that  this idea that you have like  i intend to go  so in english  uh to go as the infinitive  but uh  in uh  greek  the the you know the second verb  i intend to i go it's personalized and  the same if i remember correctly was  true in romanian  so you have these patterns so so what  might seem illogical and unlikely to  make sense in language a  absolutely makes sense in language b and  i imagine the varieties of these  potential patterns  is is very large and so really what we  have to do is not rely on any quote  logical explanation but just get used to  the pattern and i think that's best done  through a lot of listening and reading  and speaking and when you speak you  discover  where you thought you knew something and  then you don't and you go back to your  listening and reading and there's this  constant repetition of that same sort of  circular process  uh but but ultimately we have to get to  where we can use these patterns because  we've saved phrases and we've studied  phrases and we use them often enough  that they start to come out naturally  and i think that is the great advantage  of these many stories by the way the  swedish and russian first five lessons  are up at link and we're expecting more  and more all the time and by the time we  have a hundred of these  and they're fairly simple  and you need to spend your whole time on  those but it's kind of a place to go  back and practice and practice the  patterns the basic patterns of the  language and then go back to exploring  things that might be  more interesting so just to stress again  the importance of patterns in language  learning thank you for listening bye for  now  [Music]  you
 

